# Copy to `.env`. It is loaded by `src/lib/server/env.ts`, which the server, the
# scripts and drizzle-kit all import — so one file configures all of them. A real
# environment variable always wins, so a container ignores it.

# The SQLite file. One per instance; the directory is created on first run.
# Mount this path as a volume when running the container — it is the database.
DATABASE_PATH=.data/kilorep.db

# Where the generated SQL migrations live. Only set this if the folder moves;
# the server applies them at boot and the container must ship them.
# MIGRATIONS_DIR=drizzle

# Self-service sign-up at POST /api/auth/register. Off unless set to a plain
# yes (`1`, `true`, `yes`, `on`); anything else, including a typo, leaves it
# shut. You do not need this to get started — `bun run account:create` makes
# the first account locally, without exposing anything. Turn it on only for an
# instance that genuinely serves more than one person.
# ALLOW_REGISTRATION=false

# Extra origins allowed to call /api/* from a browser, comma-separated. The
# Capacitor origins are always allowed and the web surface is same-origin, so
# most instances never need this. Each entry is normalised to scheme://host:port,
# so a trailing slash is forgiven; anything that is not a URL is dropped with a
# warning on startup rather than silently matching nothing.
# CORS_ORIGINS=https://kilorep.example.com

# Behind a reverse proxy (Caddy, nginx, Traefik) these are not optional — they
# are read by adapter-node itself, and without them the server draws the wrong
# conclusion from every request rather than failing visibly:
#
#   ADDRESS_HEADER    which header carries the real client address. Unset, every
#                     request appears to come from the proxy, and the login
#                     throttle counts the whole instance as one caller — ten
#                     wrong passwords from one attacker then lock out every user
#                     for fifteen minutes, with no way to clear it early.
#   XFF_DEPTH         how many proxies you run. A client can prepend entries to
#                     x-forwarded-for, so only the last XFF_DEPTH are yours to
#                     believe; the default of 1 is right for a single proxy.
#   PROTOCOL_HEADER   without it the server sees http and leaves the session
#                     cookie un-Secure on a connection that deserved the flag.
#
# ADDRESS_HEADER=x-forwarded-for
# XFF_DEPTH=1
# PROTOCOL_HEADER=x-forwarded-proto
